Derive the following Entropy Changes During Heating and Thermal Equilibration relation from the preceding result, showing the algebra, calculus, counting, or thermodynamic identity used at each step.
\[
\Delta S_{\mathrm{res}}
=-\frac{Q}{T_R}
=-\frac{C_{\mathrm{tot}}(T_R-T_i)}{T_R}.
\tag*{[2]}
\]
Notation: \(C_{\mathrm{tot}},C_1,C_2\) denotes constant-volume total heat capacities of a body and of bodies 1 and 2; unit \(\mathrm{J K^{-1}, J K^{-1}, J K^{-1}}\); \(T_i,T_f\) denotes initial and final absolute temperatures of one heated body; unit \(\mathrm{K, K}\); \(T_1,T_2,T_{\mathrm{eq}}\) denotes initial body temperatures and their final equilibrium temperature; unit \(\mathrm{K, K, K}\); \(T_R\) denotes constant temperature of a large thermal reservoir; unit \(\mathrm{K}\); \(Q\) denotes heat transferred into the finite body; unit \(\mathrm{J}\); \(\delta Q_{\mathrm{rev}}\) denotes infinitesimal heat transferred into the body along a reversible comparison path; unit \(\mathrm{J}\); \(\Delta S,\Delta S_1,\Delta S_2\) denotes entropy changes of the body and of bodies 1 and 2; unit \(\mathrm{J K^{-1}, J K^{-1}, J K^{-1}}\); \(\Delta S_{\mathrm{res}},\Delta S_{\mathrm{univ}},\Delta S_{\mathrm{isolated}}\) denotes entropy changes of the reservoir, system plus surroundings, and an isolated composite; unit \(\mathrm{J K^{-1}, J K^{-1}, J K^{-1}}\).

Starting from the first Entropy Changes During Heating and Thermal Equilibration relation, derive the second and show the missing mathematical steps.
Start:
\[
\Delta S
=\int_{T_i}^{T_f}\frac{C_{\mathrm{tot}}\,dT}{T}
=C_{\mathrm{tot}}\ln\!\left(\frac{T_f}{T_i}\right).
\tag*{[1]}
\]

Target:
\[
\Delta S_{\mathrm{res}}
=-\frac{Q}{T_R}
=-\frac{C_{\mathrm{tot}}(T_R-T_i)}{T_R}.
\tag*{[2]}
\]
Notation: \(C_{\mathrm{tot}},C_1,C_2\) denotes constant-volume total heat capacities of a body and of bodies 1 and 2; unit \(\mathrm{J K^{-1}, J K^{-1}, J K^{-1}}\); \(T_i,T_f\) denotes initial and final absolute temperatures of one heated body; unit \(\mathrm{K, K}\); \(T_1,T_2,T_{\mathrm{eq}}\) denotes initial body temperatures and their final equilibrium temperature; unit \(\mathrm{K, K, K}\); \(T_R\) denotes constant temperature of a large thermal reservoir; unit \(\mathrm{K}\); \(Q\) denotes heat transferred into the finite body; unit \(\mathrm{J}\); \(\delta Q_{\mathrm{rev}}\) denotes infinitesimal heat transferred into the body along a reversible comparison path; unit \(\mathrm{J}\); \(\Delta S,\Delta S_1,\Delta S_2\) denotes entropy changes of the body and of bodies 1 and 2; unit \(\mathrm{J K^{-1}, J K^{-1}, J K^{-1}}\); \(\Delta S_{\mathrm{res}},\Delta S_{\mathrm{univ}},\Delta S_{\mathrm{isolated}}\) denotes entropy changes of the reservoir, system plus surroundings, and an isolated composite; unit \(\mathrm{J K^{-1}, J K^{-1}, J K^{-1}}\).
